WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.026 --> 00:00:10.868
Rebecca Clark could hardly wait to exit the small airplane.

2
00:00:11.729 --> 00:00:14.611
She got out, darted through the snow, and hugged her father.

3
00:00:15.292 --> 00:00:17.354
Rebecca was very excited to see him again.

4
00:00:18.174 --> 00:00:20.657
This was her first visit to the Arctic, where Dr.

5
00:00:20.697 --> 00:00:23.639
Clark had been working as a polar scientist for over three months.

6
00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:28.844
Rebecca knew from her father's research that the Arctic is the most northern part of the planet.

7
00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:32.974
Not many people live there because the weather is very cold most of the year.

8
00:00:33.994 --> 00:00:36.715
The Arctic has the longest winters and the shortest summers.

9
00:00:37.535 --> 00:00:39.896
It also has the longest days and the longest nights.

10
00:00:40.777 --> 00:00:45.259
In the summer, the sun shines almost all the time, and in the winter, the night can last

11
00:00:45.299 --> 00:00:46.159
up to six months.

12
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.861
Rebecca's father and his team had brought lots of fresh food with them.

13
00:00:51.561 --> 00:00:58.765
This was because the Arctic soil, called permafrost, is permanently frozen, making it impossible for trees, vegetables, or

14
00:00:58.865 --> 00:00:59.565
fruit to grow.

15
00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:05.089
The scientists had enough supplies to last them, but there was nothing Rebecca's father wanted more than a

16
00:01:05.169 --> 00:01:06.150
visit from his daughter.

17
00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:09.372
He was eager to tell her about his Arctic adventures.

18
00:01:12.114 --> 00:01:14.715
They settled in his cabin and warmed up with some tea.

19
00:01:14.735 --> 00:01:18.378
"Dad, did you make it to the North Pole?"

20
00:01:19.118 --> 00:01:19.859
asked Rebecca.

21
00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:25.563
"Yes, but I didn't walk there; I flew over it in a special airplane," he said.

22
00:01:26.063 --> 00:01:27.084
Rebecca was curious.

23
00:01:27.616 --> 00:01:29.837
"Can you walk to the North Pole?"

24
00:01:29.857 --> 00:01:36.080
"Yes," he replied, "but it is very hard to do, and many have died trying.

25
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.482
As you know, Rebecca, the North Pole is a very difficult place to visit.

26
00:01:42.022 --> 00:01:46.805
For centuries the North Pole remained one of the last unconquered places on Earth.

27
00:01:47.525 --> 00:01:52.207
People tried to see what was at the top of our planet, but they were unable to cross

28
00:01:52.247 --> 00:01:54.228
the many kilometers of ice and snow.

29
00:01:55.804 --> 00:02:02.408
In the 1500s, explorers from Holland and England were trying to find a new route to Asia, and

30
00:02:02.448 --> 00:02:06.050
they thought that going through the Arctic Ocean would shorten the way.

31
00:02:06.731 --> 00:02:11.113
A few explorers tried this route, but their ships got stuck in the ice.

32
00:02:12.814 --> 00:02:18.798
They returned home discouraged, and some explorers looked for other routes to the riches of Asia.

33
00:02:19.378 --> 00:02:21.660
Most explorers forgot about the Arctic.

34
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.541
It wasn't until the late 1800s that the golden age of Arctic exploration began."

35
00:02:27.562 --> 00:02:30.222
"Why did people try again?"

36
00:02:30.882 --> 00:02:31.583
asked Rebecca.

37
00:02:31.603 --> 00:02:39.565
"Well, even though explorers were no longer looking for a passage to Asia, they were seeking personal glory,"

38
00:02:39.945 --> 00:02:40.645
explained Dr.

39
00:02:40.685 --> 00:02:47.807
Clark. "Navigating a route through the Arctic and conquering the North Pole would give any explorer fame and

40
00:02:47.947 --> 00:02:51.068
fortune and would bring prestige to his country.

41
00:02:51.508 --> 00:02:52.828
So the race was on."

42
00:02:52.848 --> 00:02:54.529
"What do you mean?"

43
00:02:55.229 --> 00:02:55.969
asked Rebecca.

44
00:02:55.989 --> 00:03:05.292
"In 1893 the Norwegian explorer Fridtjof Nansen was the first to break through the ice and get very

45
00:03:05.372 --> 00:03:06.712
close to the North Pole.

46
00:03:07.232 --> 00:03:13.354
His famous ship, called the Fram, was especially designed for traveling through thick sea ice.

47
00:03:16.195 --> 00:03:21.612
Nansen got as far as he could, and when the Fram couldn't push through the ice anymore, he

48
00:03:21.692 --> 00:03:24.353
set out to reach the North Pole on foot.

49
00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:30.274
He took one of his crewmen with him, and they proceeded on sleds pulled by dogs.

50
00:03:31.015 --> 00:03:34.476
Exhausted after many days, they could go no further.

51
00:03:35.336 --> 00:03:44.478
They left the Norwegian flag at 86°14' N, which was the most northern latitude any explorer had ever

52
00:03:44.578 --> 00:03:50.740
reached. Fridtjof Nansen became a hero, but the North Pole had not been conquered yet."

53
00:03:51.704 --> 00:03:53.225
"So who made it first?"

54
00:03:54.106 --> 00:03:56.047
Rebecca asked as she poured more tea.

55
00:03:56.067 --> 00:03:58.949
"This is debatable," her father answered.

56
00:03:58.969 --> 00:04:02.332
"Two explorers claimed to reach the pole first.

57
00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:11.818
Robert Peary, a brave American who was obsessed with exploring the North Pole, led numerous expeditions across the

58
00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:16.021
Arctic. He finally succeeded in his quest in 1909.

59
00:04:16.801 --> 00:04:20.444
Peary learned to adapt to life in the frozen north from the Inuit people.

60
00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.943
They are often called Eskimos and have lived in the Arctic for thousands of years.

61
00:04:27.463 --> 00:04:32.846
They taught Peary how to dress warmly with sealskin and accompanied him on his expeditions.

62
00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:40.690
Finally, on his last expedition, together with his most trusted crew member, Matthew Henson, and fifty Inuit helpers,

63
00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:44.352
Peary crossed the Arctic and reached the North Pole.

64
00:04:45.473 --> 00:04:50.055
Upon his return to the United States, however, Peary learned some terrible news.

65
00:04:50.715 --> 00:04:59.271
Frederick Cook, another American explorer, was claiming that he had already reached the North Pole—one year before Peary.

66
00:04:59.851 --> 00:05:05.313
No one could prove which of the two explorers, if either of them, had actually reached the pole.

67
00:05:07.014 --> 00:05:13.417
It was tricky because back then there was no way of communicating with explorers so far north, and

68
00:05:13.437 --> 00:05:16.198
their expeditions could last a year or longer.

69
00:05:16.978 --> 00:05:21.320
Also, explorers had very primitive ways of figuring out their position.

70
00:05:21.916 --> 00:05:28.802
They used an instrument called a sextant to measure how far the sun rose above the horizon, which

71
00:05:28.882 --> 00:05:32.285
in turn told how far north or south they were.

72
00:05:33.746 --> 00:05:40.972
A sextant combines a telescope, a ruler, and a mirror, and requires skill to use accurately."

73
00:05:40.992 --> 00:05:42.634
"Then what happened?"

74
00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:43.915
asked Rebecca.

75
00:05:43.935 --> 00:05:49.360
"Both men insisted they were right, and a scandal erupted," replied Dr.

76
00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:56.020
Clark. "In the end, the National Geographic Society backed Peary's claim of reaching the North Pole.

77
00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:01.003
But even today, some still question whether Peary really succeeded."

78
00:06:01.023 --> 00:06:04.005
"Why do people doubt him?"

79
00:06:04.546 --> 00:06:05.166
asked Rebecca.

80
00:06:05.186 --> 00:06:11.831
"Robert Peary was a great explorer," explained her father, "but not a very good navigator.

81
00:06:12.391 --> 00:06:17.314
Some people thought he had read his instruments wrong when he claimed that he was at the North

82
00:06:17.394 --> 00:06:19.696
Pole." "How about you, Dad?

83
00:06:20.268 --> 00:06:22.790
What do you use when you go exploring?"

84
00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:24.130
Rebecca asked.

85
00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:30.975
"Today life in the Arctic is very different from what it was one hundred years ago," said her

86
00:06:31.015 --> 00:06:38.259
father. "We use airplanes, submarines, and special ships called icebreakers to visit the North Pole.

87
00:06:38.839 --> 00:06:42.581
Since Peary's trip, there have been hundreds of Arctic expeditions.

88
00:06:43.142 --> 00:06:46.644
We use computers and satellite telephones to keep in touch.

89
00:06:48.252 --> 00:06:56.320
Many scientists spend months in the Arctic doing tests, collecting data, and researching different scientific occurrences."

90
00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.802
"Just like you, right, Dad?"

91
00:06:59.383 --> 00:07:00.143
Rebecca said.

92
00:07:00.904 --> 00:07:05.368
She looked out the window at the pale sun, still shining in the late Arctic evening.

93
00:07:05.388 --> 00:07:08.651
"And me," she added with a wide smile.

94
00:07:09.572 --> 00:07:13.335
"I'm going to be a polar scientist when I grow up too!"
